1	% =============================================================================
2	\section{MATLAB Code}
3	% =============================================================================
4	\label{sec:matlab_appendix}
5	
6	The MATLAB implementation (shown in listing~\ref{lst:matlab_code}) is used
7	primarily to generate and manipulate the phase history data serving as input to
8	the Versal platform. Using Gorham’s SAR dataset and mathematical
9	formulation~\cite{Gorham}, 
10	
11	The MATLAB implementation (shown in Listing~\ref{lst:matlab_code}) is primarily
12	used to generate and manipulate the phase history data that serve as input to
13	the Versal platform. Using Gorham’s SAR dataset and mathematical
14	formulation~\cite{Gorham}, the MATLAB code produces post-processed iFFT'd
15	time-domain data after zero-padding in the frequency domain to achieve the
16	desired range resolution. The parameter \texttt{data.Nfft} controls the number
17	of range-frequency samples used in this process: by default it is set to 424,
18	matching the actual number of measured range samples in the dataset. Increasing
19	\texttt{Nfft} beyond 424 effectively zero-pads the spectrum, resulting in finer
20	range resolution when the data are transformed to the time domain via the iFFT.
21	This preprocessing step ensures that the data provided to the Versal hardware
22	accurately represent the intended input conditions while removing the need for
23	on-chip FFT and iFFT operations. The resulting MATLAB images also serve as
24	reference outputs for validating the accuracy of the AI Engine backprojection
25	results (shown in Figures~\ref{fig:matlab_img_360} and~\ref{fig:matlab_img_5}).
26	For example, setting \texttt{FileNum = 5} limits processing to five input
27	files, corresponding to a 5° aperture around the scene center.
28	
29	\begin{figure}[!htb]
30	  \centering
31	  \begin{subfigure}{0.48\linewidth}
32	    \centering
33	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth, trim={0 20bp 0 10bp},
34	    clip]{figures/MATLAB_GOTCHA_360Deg_424Nfft.jpg}
